WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.300
Good morning, John and Hank; it’s Friday!

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:08.140
So this October, I had my first music release
since my record came out three years ago.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:13.680
I was on tour at the time and spent the hour
before the EP came out crying,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.420
which I think was probably a surprise to my friends.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:21.589
Which, you know, it does seem like a weird
response, but those and many of my other friends

00:00:21.589 --> 00:00:25.830
also had releases coming out in the next couple
of months, and they all told me that they

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:30.009
had a similar experience the hours before
their EPs came out.

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:36.100
A few of my friends told me that releasing
something is a bit like giving birth: It’s painful,

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:42.780
which I think is like close to the—I
think that’s close to the feeling, but I

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.000
don’t think it’s quite right.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.310
I think a better analogy is it’s a bit like
sending your kid off to college.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:54.670
For a year, my art was mine to have and hold
and enjoy, and now it’s leaving and it doesn’t

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.010
belong to me anymore.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:01.490
This is especially hard if you, like many
artists, put your identity in your art.

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:02.770
What if people don’t like it?

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.680
What if people think it’s not good enough?

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.940
What if people hate me?

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.120
It’s hard to create anything, and it’s
even ha—I don’t know if it’s even

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.170
harder to put it out.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:14.350
They’re both very hard for similar reasons,
I think.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:19.619
Before you go through the execution of making
something, it exists in your head, and in

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:24.889
your head, it’s a perfect idea, perfect
execution, perfect everything.

00:01:24.889 --> 00:01:25.909
It’s beautiful.

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:31.049
But when you go through the pain of creating
something, you have to look into the face

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:32.420
of your inadequacy.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:37.860
And sometimes this is like a lack of literal hardware,
you know, you couldn’t afford something you needed.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:42.640
Or sometimes it’s: This is the first thing
I’ve ever made, and I don’t know how to do it well yet.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.640
Here’s a quote from Ira Glass about being
a beginner and an artist: “Nobody tells

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:47.999
this to the people who are beginning.

00:01:47.999 --> 00:01:49.419
I wish someone told me.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:54.400
All of us who do creative work, we get into
it because we have good taste, but there is this gap.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.079
For the first couple of years you make stuff,
it’s just not that good.

00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:01.079
It’s trying to be good, it has potential,
but it’s not.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.359
But your taste, the thing that got you in
the game, is still killer, and your taste

00:02:05.359 --> 00:02:07.639
is why your work disappoints you.”

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.240
Ira Glass, I love you!

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:16.130
So yeah, I do think that this fear of inadequacy
and this fear of maybe rejection when it comes

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:20.490
to actual release is the reason that lots
of people never create at all.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:24.410
It’s very vulnerable to say, “I made this,
and I like it.”

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:29.250
Maybe even more vulnerable to say, “I made
this, and I wanted to like it, but I don’t.”

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.760
But that’s part of learning, and that’s
a part of being a beginner.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.720
I don’t know. What's my point here?

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:41.910
I guess my point is, it’s natural to grieve
the loss of ownership and the loss of the

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.790
safety of what something was before you put
it out.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.760
And when creating, it’s natural to fear
your own inadequacy.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:50.790
But neither of those are good reasons to not
try.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.410
John, I’ll see you Friday.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:53.410
Tuesday.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:54.030
Tuesday.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.120
Oh, also, hi, my name’s Tessa Violet.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:01.090
I make video blogs and original music. Ayyy.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.980
I was on tour at the time

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:24.740
hello?

